%S A398921 0,0,0,1,1,5,5,11,15,23,23,45,45,57,73,96,96,132,132,174,198,218,218,
%T A398921 298,314,338,370,432,432,536,536,608,648,680,728,879,879,915,963,1111,
%U A398921 1111,1263,1263,1365,1485,1529,1529,1771,1807,1927,1991,2113,2113,2313
%N A398921 Number of solutions of the Diophantine inequality (a+c)*(b+d) <= n with a,b,c,d >= 1.
%C A398921 a(n) is also the number of "diamond-like" quadrilaterals of area at most n/2 whose vertices are at (-x1,0), (0,-y1), (0,y2) and (x2,0), where x1, x2, y1 and y2 are positive integers.
%C A398921 The vertices of these quadrilaterals form the pattern 2[13]2. This pattern specifies the required relative order of the y-coordinates of the vertices when read from left to right. Brackets denote points with the same x-coordinate. Thus, the left and right vertices must be in the same row, and the bottom and top vertices must be in the same column.
%C A398921 For each x,y >= 2, there are (x-1)*(y-1) quadrilaterals of width x and height y, having area x*y/2.
%C A398921 a(n) is also the number of lattice quadrilaterals of area at most n/2 whose vertices form the pattern [12]32.
%F A398921 a(n) = Sum_{x=2..floor(n/2)} (x-1)*floor(n/x)*(floor(n/x)-1)/2.
%F A398921 a(n) = n^2*log(n)/2 + (gamma - 1/4 - pi^2/6)*n^2 + o(n), where gamma is the Euler-Mascheroni constant.
%e A398921 a(6) = 5: the square with vertices {(-1,0), (0,-1), (0,1), (1,0)} has area 2; the four rotations of the quadrilateral with vertices {(-1,0), (0,-1), (0,1), (2,0)} have area 3.
%t A398921 a[n_] := Sum[(x-1)Floor[n/x](Floor[n/x]-1), {x, 2, n/2}]/2; Array[a, 54]
%Y A398921 Partial sums of A374969.
%K A398921 nonn,new
%O A398921 1,6
